cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use MIDTERMS2026 (the forecast, the poll ledger, or the derived
  tables), please cite it as below, and credit the underlying sources listed
  in DATA-RIGHTS.md.
type: dataset
title: "MIDTERMS2026: an open forecast of the 2026 U.S. midterm elections"
authors:
  - family-names: Zajac
    given-names: Kara
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0001-7400-1394"
repository-code: "https://github.com/KaraZajac/MIDTERMS2026"
license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
date-released: "2026-11-01"
keywords:
  - elections
  - forecasting
  - 2026 midterms
  - U.S. House
  - U.S. Senate
  - polling
  - Monte Carlo simulation
abstract: >-
  An open, FiveThirtyEight-style forecast of every 2026 U.S. House, Senate
  and gubernatorial race. Polls compiled from Wikipedia are averaged with
  measured house-effect, population and sponsor adjustments and weighted by
  pollster quality; House fundamentals (partisan lean on the 2026 lines,
  incumbency, fundraising) are fit on 739 contested districts from the 2018
  and 2022 midterms; expert ratings enter as a further measurement. Races
  are simulated jointly with national, regional, state and chamber-level
  correlated error. Forecasts are frozen in git before Election Day and
  scored against the results alongside the raters' consensus and naive
  baselines.
